<title>Center the content of each page</title>

 <p>Many books are typeset for printing, with a wider inner margin on every
 page. On screen the text then jumps from the left on one page to the right on
 the next. To keep it in the middle, press the menu button in the top-right
 corner of the window and select <gui style="menuitem">Center Content</gui>,
 or press <keyseq><key>M</key></keyseq>.</p>

 <p><app>Document Viewer</app> looks at what is printed on each page and moves
 it sideways so that the left and right margins are equal. The page itself
 keeps its size and position, so scrolling and zooming work as before, and you
 can still select text, follow links and add annotations.</p>

 <p>The setting is remembered for each document. To have it switched on for
 documents you have not opened before, use
 <cmd>gsettings set org.gnome.Evince.Default center-content true</cmd>.</p>

<note style="tip">
 <p>Only the horizontal position changes. A page with very little on it, such
 as a chapter opening, is centered on its own content, so it can sit slightly
 differently from the pages around it.</p>
</note>
</page>
